const [name, setName] = useState('');

<label htmlFor="name">Name</label>
<input
  id="name"
  name="name"
  value={name}
  onChange={(event) => setName(event.target.value)}
/>

body:
The handler receives a React event. `event.target` is the DOM input, so `event.target.value` is the current text. Trim only when validating; stripping spaces in `onChange` moves the cursor while the user types.

For a form with several text fields, use one state object and one handler keyed by `name`:

```jsx
const [form, setForm] = useState({ first_name: '', last_name: '' });

function handleChange(event) {
  const { name, value } = event.target;
  setForm((prev) => ({ ...prev, [name]: value }));
}
```

example:
const [email, setEmail] = useState('');

<input
  type="email"
  name="customer_email"
  value={email}
  onChange={(event) => setEmail(event.target.value)}
  autoComplete="email"
  required
/>

body:
`type="email"` gives the right mobile keyboard and lets the browser reject an address with no `@`. That check is skipped when the form has `noValidate`, and it never runs when the value comes from state without a submit, so validate in code as well:

```jsx
const clean = email.trim();
if (!/^[^@\s]+@[^@\s]+\.[^@\s]+$/.test(clean)) {
  setError('Email looks incomplete.');
}
```

A trimmed value is the one to send. Keep the raw value in state so the user sees their own typing.

example:
const [adults, setAdults] = useState('2');

function handleSubmit(event) {
  event.preventDefault();
  const count = Number(adults);
  if (!Number.isInteger(count) || count < 1) {
    setError('Whole number, at least 1.');
    return;
  }
  save({ adults: count });
}

body:
Two traps. A cleared number field gives `''`, and `Number('')` is `0`, so a required field can look like a valid zero. A partially typed value such as `-` or `1e` also reports an empty string in some browsers.

`event.target.valueAsNumber` is the alternative: it gives `NaN` for an empty or incomplete value.

```jsx
function handleChange(event) {
  const next = event.target.valueAsNumber;
  setAdults(Number.isNaN(next) ? '' : next);
}
```

Whichever route you take, do the range check with `Number.isInteger` and explicit bounds. `min` and `max` on the input only guide the spinner and the browser check.

