const [city, setCity] = useState('');

<select
  name="city"
  value={city}
  onChange={(event) => setCity(event.target.value)}
>
  <option value="">Choose a city</option>
  <option value="Budapest">Budapest</option>
  <option value="Vienna">Vienna</option>
</select>

body:
Every option needs a `value`. Without one the browser uses the option text, which breaks when the label changes or gets translated.

For a multiple select the state is an array:

```jsx
<select multiple value={cities} onChange={(event) => {
  const picked = Array.from(event.target.selectedOptions, (option) => option.value);
  setCities(picked);
}}>
```

A checkbox group is usually the better interface for several choices, because a multiple select needs a modifier key.

example:
const [agree, setAgree] = useState(false);

<label>
  <input
    type="checkbox"
    checked={agree}
    onChange={(event) => setAgree(event.target.checked)}
  />
  I accept the terms
</label>

<button type="submit" disabled={!agree}>Continue</button>

body:
Writing `value={agree}` with `event.target.value` stores the string `"on"` and the box stops following state. That is the most common mistake in exam answers.

A group of checkboxes holds an array. Toggle by building a new array:

```jsx
function toggle(list, value) {
  return list.includes(value)
    ? list.filter((item) => item !== value)
    : [...list, value];
}

setExtras((prev) => toggle(prev, 'breakfast'));
```

